        {
          "sid": "shopping-t1-s02",
          "c": "有冇平啲嘅？",
          "j": "jau5 mou5 peng4 di1 ge3?",
          "e": "Is there a cheaper one?",
          "note": "有冇 (jau5 mou5), literally \"have-not-have\", is the everyday \"is there any / do you have?\". 平 (peng4) is Cantonese for \"cheap\" (opposite of 貴 gwai3). The 嘅 (ge3) on the end makes it \"a cheaper one\" — 平啲嘅 (peng4 di1 ge3).",
          "bd": [
            {
              "c": "有冇",
              "j": "jau5 mou5",
              "e": "do you have?"
            },
            {
              "c": "平啲",
              "j": "peng4 di1",
              "e": "a bit cheaper"
            },
            {
              "c": "嘅",
              "j": "ge3",
              "e": "one (particle)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "sid": "shopping-t1-s03",
          "c": "我想買呢件。",
          "j": "ngo5 soeng2 maai5 ni1 gin6.",
          "e": "I want to buy this one.",
          "note": "Watch the tone: 買 (maai5, rising) is \"buy\", but 賣 (maai6, low falling) is \"sell\" — same character family, opposite meaning, told apart by tone alone. 件 (gin6) is the classifier for the item here.",
          "bd": [
            {
              "c": "我想",
              "j": "ngo5 soeng2",
              "e": "I want to"
            },
            {
              "c": "買",
              "j": "maai5",
